></path></svg></span></a>1. useTransition() hook</h2>
<p>By default, all updates in React are considered urgent. That could creat=
e a problem when quick updates are slowed down by heavy updates.</p>
<img src=3D"https://dmitripavlutin.com/118bc6d32d941ce629f1f18206fefc6f/leg=
acy-4.svg" alt=3D"React Legacy Rendering Mode">
<p>However, starting React 18 and the new concurrency features, you can mar=
k some updates as interruptible and non-urgent =E2=80=94 so-called transiti=
ons. That's especially useful with heavy UI updates, like filtering a big l=
ist.</p>
<img src=3D"https://dmitripavlutin.com/2539ba977c8a8880be97d01c03835a88/con=
current-4.svg" alt=3D"React Concurrent Rendering Mode">
<p><code>useTransition()</code> is the hook that lets you access concurrent=
 mode features inside of the React component.</p>
<p>Invoking <code>const [isPending, startTransition] =3D useTransitionHook(=
)</code> returns an array of 2 items:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>isPending</code>: indicates that the transition is pending</li>
<li><code>startTransition(callback)</code>: allows you to mark any UI updat=
es inside <code>callback</code> as transitions.</li>
</ul>
